  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 125 AM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nSaturday, the upper-level ridge will move eastward into north Texas, \nas an upper trough moves across the Pacific Northwest. This will \nbring a west-southwesterly flow aloft and a downsloping \nsouthwesterly surface wind and above normal temperatures. Saturday \nwill be the warmest day, reaching the mid 90s west, with upper 90s \nnear 100 across the southwest. Dry conditions expected during \nthe day. With a southwest wind 10 to 15 mph and gusts to 25 mph \nat times across the western Sandhills along with afternoon \nhumidity falling to as low as 20 to 25 percent, elevated fire \nweather conditions are expected mainly west of Highway 61 \nSaturday afternoon. A weak cold frontal passage Saturday night, \nwith low chances for showers/storms. Sunday, highs slightly \ncooler behind the front in the upper 80s, with 90 to 95 \nsouthwest. The surface front is forecast to extend from \nsouthwest Kansas into southeast Nebraska. Low chances again for \nshowers/storms Sunday night, mainly across the west and south.\n\nA longwave trough moving through the West Coast of the U.S. into the \ninterior West and upper ridge extending across the Southern Plains \nand southeast U.S. will bring an increased southwesterly flow aloft \nto the region Monday through Wednesday. A surface front may remain \nstalled out across southwest Kansas into southeast Nebraska Monday \nand Tuesday, providing a better chance for showers and storms Monday \nnight, and again Tuesday night. High temperatures moderate to 85 to \n90. By Wednesday, a cold front and shortwave trough is forecast to \nmove east across the Northern and Central Plains, with at least a \nslight chance for showers/storms.\n\nDespite daily precipitation chances, meaningful rainfall is not \nexpected Saturday through Monday morning. NBM 48 hour probabilities \nfor at least 0.25\" ending Monday morning is only 5 to 15 percent. \nProbabilities increase to 25 to 35 percent area wide for the 48 hour \nperiod ending Wednesday morning, when wetting rains appear more \npromising.",
